include "../compiler/runtime/elisacore_std/elisacore_runtime.elisa"

def main() -> i64:
    can Console.Write, Memory.Allocate, Console.Format, Abort.Panic:
        print(42)
        return 0

# stage1 declines this unit; stage0 compiles and runs it. STILL OPEN.
#
#   error: backend emitted no functions for this unit;
#          declined 5: join, print__i64, print, print__u32, print
#
# Progress since first filed: the count was 40 declined store_* instantiations,
# now 5. The generic work done since then fixed most of it.
#
# The declining statement is the BODY of the generic print:
#
#   print: DECLINE Expr @line 7092
#     -> runtime.elisa:271   print(T.__cast__(value) can ...) can Console.Write
#
# NEGATIVE RESULTS -- these were each reduced and BOTH STAGES AGREE, so none of
# them is the cause. Do not re-investigate them:
#
#   1. Protocol dispatch through a TYPE PARAMETER (`T.method(value)` rather than
#      `value.method()`). Both spellings, both stages: correct.
#      See tparam.elisa in the session scratchpad; the shape is
#          protocol Describe: def describe(self: Self) -> i64
#          def via_type_param[T: Describe](value: T) -> i64: return T.describe(value)
#
#   2. A GENERIC calling a same-named NON-GENERIC OVERLOAD -- the exact shape of
#      `print[T: Str]` calling `print(cstr)` -- with the protocol method
#      returning cstr and carrying can[Memory.Allocate, Console.Format,
#      Abort.Panic]. Both stages print 42. This was the strongest hypothesis
#      (that stage1 resolves the inner call back to the generic rather than the
#      overload) and it is WRONG.
#
#   3. The effect clauses on the protocol method and the call. Carried in the
#      reduction at (2); no effect.
#
# What is still unexplained, and is where to look next:
#
#   * `print__u32` is instantiated at all. `print(42)` needs one integer
#      instantiation, not a u32 one -- so something else in the concatenated
#      runtime is dragging a second monomorphization in, and the decline may
#      belong to THAT one rather than to the i64 one.
#   * `join` declines independently in the same unit (Assign/For/If inside a
#      runtime function, lines 6403-6406) and would drop the unit on its own.
#      It does NOT decline for other programs against the same runtime, so it is
#      instantiation-dependent too. Fixing print without fixing join leaves the
#      unit dropped, so measure both.
#
# NOT a blocker for the port: the differential sweeps format integers by hand
# (see the printr note at the top of test/board_sweep.elisa) precisely because of
# this, and every suite passes on stage1 without it.
